    "turn_text": "When I got to the Senate, we were really bipartisan on climate. Senator McCain was the Republican nominee. He had a great climate platform. I count in the years seven, eight, and nine, my first three years in the Senate. Five different bipartisan Senate climate bills that all would have done something really significant. They weren't little nibly around the edges, Bill. They were real, strong, great pieces of legislation by bipartisan hearings, bipartisan conversations. I'm married to a scientist, I believe in science. I'm exposed to nature a lot. I know very well what's going on. So when 2010 came and all of that came to a dead stop, to me that was very, very frustrating. I think the Citizens United decision gave immense political weaponry to the fossil fuel industry, which they brought instantly to bear to shut down all that bipartisanship. And to my frustration, the Obama administration walked away from the cap and trade bill, told the Senate to not even do an empty bill that could have gotten us at a conference to see what could have been done by partisan, walked away, and you couldn't get the White House to put the words climate and change in the same paragraph. And so, deeply frustrated, I started making these weekly speeches, I told my staff, look, if I have to do this without you scheduling me regularly, then we'll always fall off the table. So every single damned week that the Senate's in session, I'm gonna give a speech on climate. So get me out there. And so where I'm coming up on number 250, I wish I'd been able to stop at 150 or 50, but the fundamental problem I think remains that there's a very powerful industry that doesn't want this to happen. Now the good news is that at the top, that industry has changed its position. If you look at what the CEOs say, they know it's a problem, they know their product is causing it, and they know that a carbon price is the best, most efficient and effective solution. The problem is that that word hasn't gotten down to all of the different groups that they have propped up over the years to do their business. So there's a, there's a bit of a lag between what the CEOs say and what the technical tips of the beast are still doing. And I don't know if this is, you remember the movie Fantasia?",
